# How to Run

## Requirements

Make sure the following are installed:

- `g++`
- `make`
- C++17-compatible compiler

Check:

```bash
g++ --version
make --version
```

---

## 1. Build the Project

Open a terminal in the project root:

```bash
cd 32-bit-RISC-V-CPU-Simulator
```

Build all components:

```bash
make
```

A successful build creates three executables in the `build/` directory:

```text
build/
├── riscv_cpu
├── cache_simulator
└── trace_generator
```

You can verify them with:

```bash
ls -lh build/
```

---

# 2. After the build succeeds, you can run the three components below.

| Executable | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `riscv_cpu` | Runs the 32-bit RISC-V CPU simulator |
| `trace_generator` | Generates a memory-access trace |
| `cache_simulator` | Simulates the cache using a memory-access trace |

---

## 2.1 Run the RISC-V CPU Simulator

The CPU simulator uses the sample assembly program:

```text
examples/input.txt
```

Run:

```bash
./build/riscv_cpu
```

The simulator displays:

- Instruction fetch
- Instruction decode
- Execute stage
- Write-back
- Pipeline stalls
- Branch/jump handling
- Register values
- Final register state

The included example calculates:

```text
6! = 720
```

So the final result should include:

```text
x2 = 720
```

No additional arguments are required.

---

## 2.2 Generate a Memory-Access Trace

The cache simulator requires a memory-access trace.

Use the trace generator to create one.

### Syntax

```bash
./build/trace_generator N M stride element_size output_file
```

### Example

```bash
./build/trace_generator 64 64 1 4 examples/example_trace.txt
```

This generates a matrix-multiplication-style memory-access trace and writes it to:

```text
examples/example_trace.txt
```

After generating the trace, you can check it with:

```bash
head examples/example_trace.txt
```

The trace generator arguments are:

| Argument | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `N` | First matrix dimension |
| `M` | Second matrix dimension |
| `stride` | Memory access stride |
| `element_size` | Element size in bytes |
| `output_file` | Output trace file |

You may also provide your own compatible trace file instead of generating one.

---

## 2.3 Run the Cache Simulator

Once a trace has been generated, run the cache simulator.

The simulator uses:

```text
config/config.cfg
```

for the cache configuration and:

```text
examples/example_trace.txt
```

as the memory-access trace.

Run:

```bash
./build/cache_simulator config/config.cfg examples/example_trace.txt
```

The simulator reports results for:

- Direct-Mapped + Write-Through
- Direct-Mapped + Write-Back
- Fully Associative + Write-Through
- Fully Associative + Write-Back
- 4-Way Set Associative + Write-Through
- 4-Way Set Associative + Write-Back

For each configuration, the output includes statistics such as:

```text
Accesses
Hits
Misses
Miss Rate
Writebacks
MemBytes
Cycles
AMAT
Bytes/Cycle
```

---

# 3. Complete Example

To run the complete project from start to finish:

```bash
make
```

Then run the CPU simulator:

```bash
./build/riscv_cpu
```

Generate a memory trace:

```bash
./build/trace_generator 64 64 1 4 examples/example_trace.txt
```

Finally, run the cache simulator:

```bash
./build/cache_simulator config/config.cfg examples/example_trace.txt
```

The CPU simulator is independent and does not require the trace generator.

The trace generator and cache simulator are used together:

```text
Trace Generator
      ↓
example_trace.txt
      ↓
Cache Simulator
```

---

# 4. Build Individual Components

You do not need to rebuild the entire project every time.

Build only the RISC-V CPU simulator:

```bash
make cpu
```

Build only the cache simulator:

```bash
make cache
```

Build only the trace generator:

```bash
make trace
```

---

# 5. Clean the Build

To remove all generated executables:

```bash
make clean
```

Then rebuild everything:

```bash
make
```

---

# 6. Makefile Help

To see the available build commands:

```bash
make help
```

Available targets:

```text
make        Build all executables
make cpu    Build RISC-V CPU simulator
make cache  Build cache simulator
make trace  Build trace generator
make clean  Remove build files
make help   Show available commands
```

---

# Quick Start

For someone using the project for the first time:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Divyansh9778/32-bit-RISC-V-CPU-Simulator
cd 32-bit-RISC-V-CPU-Simulator

make

./build/riscv_cpu

./build/trace_generator 64 64 1 4 examples/example_trace.txt

./build/cache_simulator config/config.cfg examples/example_trace.txt
```

That's the complete workflow.
